1. Non-negotiable locks
Lock A — finished shrinking only
Shrinking is instantaneous and is never shown in progress. Do not depict an intermediate height, morphing anatomy, a lingering beam, glow, distortion, or other visual residue. The scene begins after the size change is complete.


Lock B — adulthood never changes
Shrinking changes physical scale only. The person remains the exact same adult: same chronological age, identity, facial structure, mature anatomy, adult skeletal plan, and adult head-to-body ratio. Never introduce child, infant, toddler, doll-like, dwarfism-like, or stumpy traits.


Lock C — stated sizes never drift
A stated size is exact and fixed until a new size is explicitly given. One inch, one centimeter, and one meter have one meaning. No rounding, approximation, silent resizing, panel-to-panel fluctuation, or camera-based size change is permitted.


Lock D — the world never rescales
The environment remains ordinary full-scale reality. Objects, tools, furnishings, surfaces, rooms, and distances keep their true real-world dimensions. Nothing shrinks, enlarges, shifts, bends, or rearranges itself to make the small person easier to depict.


2. The 1:1 world never adapts
The 1:1 world is full-scale reality, and only the human is shrunk. Every object, surface, furnishing, and environment remains the size it would be in ordinary life. The world is made for normal-sized humans and never accommodates the smaller person through substitution, convenient miniaturization, or spatial distortion.
Real units remain authoritative. A measurement written in inches, centimeters, meters, or another International System of Units (SI) unit is literal.
Objects keep their dimensions, spacing, mass, material behavior, and relationships to one another.
A wrong-looking scale is corrected by fixing the person, camera construction, or written measurement—not by changing the world.
The environment remains coherent when the small person is mentally removed from the frame; it is not a miniature set or a backdrop built around them.
Only explicitly stated changes may alter the environment. Nothing moves between panels merely to simplify composition.
One-sentence rule
Shrink the person, not the world.


3. Adult anatomy remains uniform
Apply one uniform scale factor to the entire adult body. Preserve mature anatomy and the same relative measurements from crown to sole. “Same head size” means the same adult head-to-body proportion after uniform scaling—not an unchanged absolute head dimension.
Maintain an adult figure of approximately eight head-lengths from crown to sole, unless a specifically established character reference requires another adult proportion.
Scale the head, neck, torso, shoulders, arms, hands, pelvis, hips, thighs, knees, lower legs, and feet by the identical factor.
Preserve the person’s recognizable identity, mature facial structure, build, body distribution, skin, hair, and other established physical traits.
Do not shorten the limbs, compress the torso, enlarge the head, broaden the hands or feet, or redistribute body mass to make the figure fit the frame.
Do not simplify anatomy because the person is small. Biological detail remains as complete as it is at normal size.
Uniform scaling test
Let H₀ be the person’s original height and H₁ the stated shrunk height. The scale factor is s = H₁ ÷ H₀. Every linear body dimension must become its original value multiplied by s. No body part receives a different factor.
4. Measurements are literal
Use exact numbers and units wherever scale matters. Avoid adjectives such as “tiny,” “small,” or “miniature” when they are asked to carry dimensional meaning. Those words may describe an impression, but they never replace a measurement.
State the current height in every panel where scale could otherwise be misread.
Write one exact value and its unit. Add an exact conversion when useful.
Carry the same number through all panels, sentences, continuations, and camera changes until an explicit new size is given.
Use comparisons only as secondary orientation. Approximate everyday objects do not define the person’s size.
Check all distances, reaches, supports, and actions against the stated height before writing them.
Remove words such as “approximately,” “about,” or “roughly” from the person’s stated size unless the user explicitly requests an estimate.
Exact seven-inch reference
Seven inches equals exactly 17.78 centimeters because one inch equals exactly 2.54 centimeters. At an eight-heads-tall proportion, each head-length is 0.875 inch, or 2.2225 centimeters.
