5. Camera changes viewpoint only
Camera placement changes what is visible, not what is true. Every angle and shift must preserve the same body, size, environment, geometry, behavior, and causal state.
Do not use forced perspective, fisheye distortion, barrel distortion, peripheral stretching, or exaggerated wide-angle rendering.
Avoid viewpoints that make the head, hands, feet, limbs, or torso appear to change size relative to the rest of the body.
Foreshortening may occur naturally, but it must remain anatomically legible and must not read as a shortened or missing limb.
A high, low, near, far, or side view must preserve the subject’s exact stated height and the world’s fixed scale.
Camera shifts must not reset lighting, furnishings, positions, expressions, attention, or action continuity.
No angle may sacrifice plausibility, facial language, body language, or spatial awareness to create a dramatic composition.
Camera integrity test
If the scene were observed from the opposite side, every real measurement and spatial relationship would remain identical. Only visibility and overlap would change.


6. Panels progress as one continuous event
A tych is one panel. Multi-panel scenes use a single horizontal left-to-right sequence that fits within one 16:9 image. Do not use a 2×2 grid, vertical stack, split above-and-below layout, or extra unrequested panels.
Each panel advances the same event naturally; do not repeat or recycle earlier scene beats.
Restate the current exact size with intact proportions whenever a panel might otherwise permit scale drift.
Preserve the same environment, furniture, objects, people, attire, lighting logic, and time conditions unless the story visibly changes them.
Every new position, reaction, object movement, or physical effect must follow from a visible or clearly stated cause.
Do not teleport people or objects, reset posture, change identity, or introduce unrequested participants between panels.
Use distinct, coherent camera views only when they improve spatial understanding without changing scale or anatomy.
Keep panel transitions readable without decorative borders, labels, or written text beyond necessary dialogue or panel numbering.
7. Humans remain living and behaviorally credible
Every depicted person is a living, sentient adult with full biological detail and active awareness of the scene. Small scale never turns a person into a prop, figurine, toy, or passive visual marker.
Biological detail
Retain natural skin texture, pores, fine lines, color variation, body heat cues, and soft-tissue response.
Render hair as individual strands and masses that respond to gravity, motion, contact, and airflow; never as a molded helmet.
Keep eyes moist and dimensional, hands anatomically complete, joints credible, and facial features fully resolved.
Show breathing, balance, muscle engagement, tension, fatigue, or relaxation when the action calls for it.
Apply realistic folds, compression, tissue displacement, and contact response wherever bodies meet clothing, surfaces, or hands.
Facial and body language
Use natural asymmetry rather than bilateral mirroring: shoulders and pelvic line should not read as perfectly level and parallel.
Give the torso subtle spinal curvature and rotational torsion; avoid flat front-facing alignment.
Assign the left and right limbs different, situation-specific tasks. Do not mirror the arms, hands, legs, or feet.
Vary finger articulation and hand angle so hands look engaged rather than posed.
Use asymmetric micro-expression: uneven brow tension, slight unilateral mouth movement, relaxed jaw variation, and gaze that is not locked to the nose axis.
Match expression, attention, balance, and effort to what the person knows, sees, feels, and can physically do at that exact scale.
Avoid T-poses, A-poses, soldier stances, mannequin rigidity, passport symmetry, frozen catalog posing, and theatrical overreaction.
8. Contact, support, and matter are tangible
Every body and object must have a credible load path. Nothing floats, clips, phases through matter, or rests on an invisible support. Contact is visible through pressure, deformation, overlap, shadow, and posture.
Every hand, limb, and torso rests fully on top of or against a solid surface when contact is claimed.
Show contact shadows and ambient occlusion at every supporting point.
Rigid surfaces resist; soft surfaces compress, fold, spread, or rebound according to material and load.
Body weight changes joint angles, muscle tension, balance, and the support surface’s response.
Water, fabric, hair, and loose objects obey gravity and momentum; they do not remain stiff or weightless.
No random body part, shadow, limb, duplicate figure, or off-screen anatomy may appear without a single accountable person and position.
Supported grasp handling
When a larger person holds a small adult, use a broad, enclosing grasp rather than a fingertip pinch. The palm, fingers, and opposing thumb distribute support around the torso or midsection. The hold must read as careful human handling, not object manipulation.
Use broad contact across the palm and finger pads; never suspend the person by fingertips, clothing, or a limb.
Show realistic but non-injurious soft-tissue compression at contact points and visible grip effort in the holder’s hand and wrist.
The held person’s legs, head, torso, arms, hair, and clothing respond to gravity and motion.
The held person remains aware and reactive, with a plausible brace, counter-grip, posture, gaze, and expression.
No gap may imply floating inside the grasp; no perfect figurine-like standing pose may substitute for balance.
9. Rendering language is documentary and specific
The target look is authentic, in-color documentary photography: candid, naturally lit, physically grounded, and unretouched in appearance. The scene should read as a real event captured by an ordinary camera rather than as an illustration or manufactured miniature.
Use unified natural lighting, coherent shadow direction, realistic grain, and consistent exposure across panels.
Keep skin, hair, clothing, surfaces, and materials detailed rather than smoothed or simplified.
Exclude digital-render, computer-generated imagery (CGI), 3D-model, illustration, painting, airbrush, plastic, wax, resin, porcelain, clay, or toy aesthetics.
Exclude golden-hour stylization, staged lighting, clinical presentation, filters, black-and-white treatment, and artificial drama.
Exclude diorama, dollhouse, tabletop miniature, tilt-shift, action-figure, and figurine cues.
Avoid generic attire and automatic wardrobe defaults. Clothing must be specifically described, consistent, and physically responsive; do not default to denim.
Never use first-person hands. Keep the camera unacknowledged by the subjects unless the story explicitly requires acknowledgment.
